\paragraph{An exact first nested integral.} For $0\leq z<1$, the source gives $I(z)=-\log(1-z)$ and
\[
 I_\bullet(z)=\int_0^1\frac{z[-\log(1-x)]}{1-zx}\,dx.
\]
Expanding the nonnegative geometric series and integrating termwise gives $I_\bullet(z)=\sum_{m\geq1}H_mz^m/m$, where $H_m=\sum_{k=1}^m1/k$. To verify the coefficient, write $-\log(1-x)=\sum_{k\geq1}x^k/k$ and use $\sum_{k\geq1}1/[k(k+m)]=H_m/m$, a telescoping difference.

\paragraph{Reduction to familiar functions.} Define $\operatorname{Li}_2(z)=\sum_{m\geq1}z^m/m^2$. Differentiating convergent series on compact subsets of $[0,1)$ gives
\[
 \frac d{dz}\sum_{m\geq1}\frac{H_m}{m}z^m
 =\frac{-\log(1-z)}{z(1-z)}.
\]
The same derivative is obtained from $\operatorname{Li}_2(z)+\tfrac12\log^2(1-z)$, and both vanish at zero. Thus $I_\bullet(z)=\operatorname{Li}_2(z)+\tfrac12\log^2(1-z)$. The logarithmic divergence at $z\uparrow1$ shows that endpoint substitutions need explicit renormalized constants rather than unqualified evaluation.

